-- Yoga-for-Burnout
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Burnout is not ordinary tiredness. It is what is left when you have been running on effort alone for too long: sleep stops restoring you, small tasks feel heavy, and the things you used to care about go quiet. Yoga will not remove whatever caused it, but it is one of the few practices that teaches a depleted body how to rest again.</p><section id="what-burnout-is"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Burnout Actually Is</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Burnout usually builds slowly, out of long stretches of pressure with no real recovery in between. It tends to show up in three ways at once: <strong>exhaustion</strong> that sleep does not fix, <strong>distance</strong> from work or people you once cared about, and a creeping sense that nothing you do is quite enough.</p><p>It is different from a hard week. A hard week ends and you bounce back. Burnout is the point where the bounce has gone, because the system that normally recovers has been switched on for so long that it has forgotten how to switch off.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Exhaustion that rest does not fix</li><li>Feeling detached or numb about things that used to matter</li><li>Small decisions feeling disproportionately hard</li></ul></div></section><section id="why-rest-stops-working"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Why Rest Stops Working</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">When you live under constant demand, the body&rsquo;s stress response stays partly switched on, even on the sofa, even in bed. You may be lying still while your heart rate, breathing and muscle tone are still braced for the next thing. That is why a weekend off often barely touches burnout: the body was never actually resting.</p><p>Recovery needs the opposite state, the slower, softer mode in which digestion, repair and deep sleep happen. For many burnt-out people that state has to be relearned, deliberately, a few minutes at a time.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>How Yoga Helps You Recover</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The yoga that helps burnout is not the yoga that pushes. A strong, sweaty class can feel like one more thing to perform. What helps is practice that lowers the load:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Slow, supported postures</strong> that let the body stop holding itself up</li><li><strong>Longer exhalations</strong>, which are one of the simplest signals of safety you can give your nervous system</li><li><strong>Yoga Nidra</strong>, a guided practice lying down that many people find gives them the first real rest in months</li><li><strong>Gentle movement</strong> that reconnects you with the body without asking anything of it</li></ul><p>None of this needs to be long. Ten quiet minutes done most days does more than one heroic session a week.</p></section><section id="a-gentle-practice"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Gentle 15-Minute Practice</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Legs up the wall (5 minutes):</strong> lie on your back with your legs resting against a wall and your arms by your sides.</li><li><strong>Supported child&rsquo;s pose (3 minutes):</strong> knees wide, a cushion or folded blanket under your chest and head.</li><li><strong>Breathing with a longer exhale (3 minutes):</strong> breathe in for a count of four, out for a count of six, without straining.</li><li><strong>Rest (4 minutes):</strong> lie flat with a blanket over you and let the body be heavy.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you notice guilt about resting, that is common in burnout. Treat the practice as an appointment, not a reward you have to earn.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How long does it take to recover from burnout?</h3><p>It varies a great deal. Mild burnout can ease within weeks once the pressure drops and rest becomes real; deeper burnout often takes months. Recovery is rarely a straight line, so expect good and bad days.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is hot or power yoga good for burnout?</h3><p>Usually not at first. Intense, demanding classes can feel like another performance and leave you more drained. Start with gentle, restorative and breath-led practice, and add stronger work later if you want it.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can I do yoga for burnout at my desk?</h3><p>Yes. Slow breathing with a longer exhale, shoulder rolls, seated twists and a minute of closing your eyes between tasks all count. Small, frequent pauses help more than you might expect.</p></section><section id="beyond-the-mat"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Recovery Beyond the Mat</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga works best alongside changes to whatever is draining you: protecting sleep, saying no to some demands, eating regularly, and spending time with people who do not need anything from you.</p><p>Burnout can also overlap with depression and anxiety. If you feel hopeless, cannot function day to day, or the exhaustion is not lifting over several weeks, please speak to a doctor or a mental health professional. Yoga is a good companion to that support, not a replacement for it.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;Burnout is not a failure of effort. It is a body that needs to be taught, gently, that it is allowed to stop.&rdquo;</p></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Burnout and Emotional Exhaustion: Rest and Recover', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Yoga-for-Burnout' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Yoga-for-Overthinking
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Overthinking feels like a mind that will not put anything down. You replay a conversation, rehearse one that has not happened, and lie awake running the same loop. Telling yourself to stop rarely works. What does work, for a lot of people, is giving the mind something steadier to hold, and yoga is built around exactly that.</p><section id="why-the-mind-loops"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why the Mind Gets Stuck in Loops</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Overthinking is usually the mind trying to protect you. It goes back over the past to find what went wrong and runs ahead into the future to prepare for what might. The trouble is that it does not know when to stop, and the more tired or anxious you are, the louder the loop gets.</p><p>Thoughts also feed on the body. A tight chest, shallow breathing and tense shoulders tell the brain something is wrong, and the brain goes looking for what it is. Calm the body and the search often quietens with it.</p></section><section id="attention-not-argument"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Attention, Not Argument</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">You cannot argue a racing mind into silence. Yoga takes a different route: it moves your attention from the content of the thoughts to something physical and present, such as the breath, a sensation, or the shape of a posture.</p><p>Each time the mind wanders and you notice and come back, you strengthen the skill that overthinking lacks: the ability to step out of a thought rather than follow it. That skill grows with practice, like any other.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Do not fight the thoughts; redirect attention</li><li>The breath is always available as an anchor</li><li>Noticing that you have drifted is the practice, not a failure of it</li></ul></div></section><section id="practices-that-help"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Practices That Help</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Breath counting:</strong> count each exhale from one to ten, then start again. When you lose count, simply begin at one.</li><li><strong>Alternate nostril breathing (Nadi Shodhana):</strong> slow and rhythmic, it gives restless attention a clear, simple task.</li><li><strong>Balancing postures</strong> like tree pose: it is very hard to balance and ruminate at the same time.</li><li><strong>Body scan:</strong> move attention slowly from the feet to the head, noticing without changing anything.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If sitting still makes the thoughts louder, move first. A few rounds of gentle sun salutations before sitting can settle restless energy.</p></div></section><section id="a-routine-for-busy-minds"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Short Routine for Busy Minds</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Try this in the evening, when overthinking often peaks: five minutes of slow movement, three minutes of alternate nostril breathing, then five minutes lying down with a body scan. Keep your phone in another room while you do it.</p><p>Writing the loop down before you begin can also help. Once a worry is on paper, the mind is often more willing to let it rest for a while.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">What is the best time of day to practise for overthinking?</h3><p>Whenever your mind is busiest. For many people that is the evening or bedtime, so a short practice before sleep is a good place to start. A few minutes in the morning can also set a calmer tone for the day.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is meditation better than yoga for overthinking?</h3><p>They work well together. Movement can settle a restless body so that sitting still becomes easier, and meditation trains attention more directly. Many people find a few minutes of each works better than either alone.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">What if my thoughts get louder when I try to relax?</h3><p>That is very common. Try moving first, keep sessions short, and use a counting practice to give the mind a clear task. If quiet consistently makes you feel worse, it is worth talking to a professional.</p></section><section id="when-to-get-help"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When Overthinking Needs More Support</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Some overthinking is simply part of being human. But if it is constant, stops you sleeping, or comes with panic, low mood or intrusive thoughts you cannot shake, it may be part of an anxiety condition that deserves proper care. A doctor or therapist can help, and yoga sits well alongside that help.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;You do not have to win against your thoughts. You only have to keep coming back to the breath.&rdquo;</p></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Overthinking: Quiet Racing Thoughts and Restlessness', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Yoga-for-Overthinking' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Yoga-Helps-Your-Gut
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Yoga is often sold as a cure-all for digestion: twist here, detox there, and your gut will sort itself out. The truth is more modest and more useful. Yoga can genuinely help digestion, mostly by calming the stress that disrupts it, but it cannot replace good food, sleep or medical care when something is wrong.</p><section id="what-yoga-can-do"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Yoga Can Genuinely Do</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Your gut is closely connected to your nervous system. When you are stressed, digestion is one of the first things the body puts on hold. That is why worry can show up as cramps, bloating, or a sudden change in bowel habits.</p><p>Yoga&rsquo;s main gift to the gut is helping the body back into its rest-and-digest state. Slow breathing, gentle movement and real relaxation make it easier for digestion to do its job. Movement itself can also help things move along, which many people with sluggish digestion notice.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Calms the stress response that disrupts digestion</li><li>Gentle movement can ease gas and support regularity</li><li>Encourages slower eating and better awareness of the body</li></ul></div></section><section id="what-it-cannot-do"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What It Cannot Do</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga does not &lsquo;detox&rsquo; your organs, and twisting postures do not wring toxins out of the liver; your liver and kidneys do that job continuously. Yoga cannot cure food intolerances, coeliac disease, inflammatory bowel disease or infections, and it will not make up for a diet that does not suit you.</p><p>Being honest about this matters, because believing yoga is enough can delay a diagnosis that really is needed.</p></section><section id="helpful-practices"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Practices Worth Trying</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Knees-to-chest (Apanasana):</strong> lie on your back and hug the knees in, rocking gently.</li><li><strong>Gentle supine twist:</strong> let both knees fall to one side, then the other.</li><li><strong>Cat-cow:</strong> slow movement of the spine that also gently massages the abdomen.</li><li><strong>Belly breathing:</strong> a hand on the abdomen, letting it rise and fall with each breath.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Avoid strong twists and inversions straight after a big meal. Gentle practice an hour or two after eating is usually most comfortable.</p></div></section><section id="the-bigger-picture"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>The Bigger Picture for Gut Health</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga works best alongside the basics: regular meals eaten slowly, enough fibre and water, decent sleep, and fewer ultra-processed foods. Many people find that how they eat (rushed, stressed, in front of a screen) matters almost as much as what they eat.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Do yoga twists detox the body?</h3><p>No. Twists can feel good and may help with gas and comfort, but detoxification is done continuously by the liver and kidneys. There is no need to &lsquo;wring out&rsquo; toxins.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Which yoga is best for digestion?</h3><p>Gentle styles with slow movement and plenty of breathing, such as Hatha or restorative yoga, tend to suit digestion best. Avoid vigorous practice soon after eating.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga help IBS?</h3><p>Many people with irritable bowel syndrome find that yoga helps them manage stress-related flare-ups and feel more at ease in their bodies. It works best alongside the dietary and medical advice your doctor gives you.</p></section><section id="see-a-doctor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to See a Doctor</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Please see a doctor rather than relying on yoga if you notice blood in your stool, unexplained weight loss, persistent vomiting, severe or worsening pain, difficulty swallowing, or any lasting change in bowel habits, especially after the age of 50. These need proper investigation.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;Yoga can help your gut feel safe enough to work. It cannot do the work of a diagnosis.&rdquo;</p></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga Helps Your Gut, But It''s Not Magic: An Honest Guide', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Yoga-Helps-Your-Gut' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Bloated-Again
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Bloating is one of the most common digestive complaints there is, and one of the most frustrating: the tight, swollen feeling that can turn a good day uncomfortable by mid-afternoon. Most bloating is harmless and has ordinary causes. A few simple changes, and some gentle yoga, can make a real difference.</p><section id="common-causes"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why You Keep Getting Bloated</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Bloating usually comes from gas, slow movement through the gut, or a sensitive gut that notices normal amounts of gas more than most. Common triggers include:</p><ul class="art-list"><li>Eating quickly and swallowing air</li><li>Fizzy drinks and chewing gum</li><li>Large meals, especially late in the evening</li><li>Certain foods that ferment easily, such as beans, onions or some fruits</li><li>Constipation</li><li>Stress, which changes how the gut moves and how sensitive it feels</li></ul></section><section id="what-helps-now"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Helps Right Now</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">When you are already bloated, the aim is to help trapped gas move and to stop tensing against the discomfort.</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>A slow walk</strong> for ten to fifteen minutes is one of the most effective things you can do.</li><li><strong>Warm drinks</strong> such as ginger or fennel tea feel soothing for many people.</li><li><strong>Loosen tight clothing</strong> and avoid lying flat straight after eating.</li><li><strong>Breathe into the belly</strong> rather than holding it in.</li></ul></section><section id="yoga-for-bloating"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Gentle Yoga for Bloating</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">These postures are gentle enough to do when you feel uncomfortable:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Wind-relieving pose (Pavanamuktasana):</strong> on your back, hug one knee in, then the other, then both.</li><li><strong>Child&rsquo;s pose with knees wide:</strong> lets the belly soften between the thighs.</li><li><strong>Cat-cow:</strong> a few slow rounds to encourage movement.</li><li><strong>Gentle supine twist:</strong> both knees to one side, breathe, then switch.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Keep everything slow and comfortable. Pressing hard into an already sore belly does not help.</p></div></section><section id="prevent-it"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Preventing the Next Round</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Eat more slowly, chew well, and try smaller meals. Notice whether particular foods reliably set you off; a simple food and symptom diary for two weeks often reveals a pattern. Keep moving through the day, and give stress a regular outlet. Many people find that a short breathing practice before meals helps them eat more calmly.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Slow down meals</li><li>Walk after eating</li><li>Track your triggers before cutting foods out</li><li>Manage stress, not just diet</li></ul></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How quickly can yoga relieve bloating?</h3><p>Gentle movement and a short walk often bring some relief within minutes to an hour, by helping trapped gas move. Lasting improvement usually comes from changes to how and what you eat.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is bloating every day normal?</h3><p>Mild bloating after some meals is common. Bloating that is new, persistent or getting worse, especially with other symptoms, should be checked by a doctor.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I cut out foods to stop bloating?</h3><p>Try tracking your meals and symptoms before cutting foods out. Removing many foods at once can make it harder to eat well and to find the real trigger; a dietitian can help if you suspect a pattern.</p></section><section id="when-to-worry"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When Bloating Needs Checking</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">See a doctor if bloating is persistent and new, especially if you are over 50, or if it comes with weight loss, a change in bowel habits, blood in the stool, ongoing pain, or feeling full very quickly. These are worth having looked at properly rather than managing on your own.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Bloated Again? What Your Gut Actually Needs Right Now', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Bloated-Again' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Your-Bowels-Have
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Your digestion runs on rhythm. The gut is most active at certain times of day, and it responds to regular meals, movement and sleep. When life becomes irregular, from travel and shift work to rushed mornings and stress, that rhythm gets lost, and constipation or unpredictable bowels often follow. The good news is that rhythm can be rebuilt.</p><section id="the-gut-clock"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>The Gut Has a Clock</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Like the rest of the body, the gut follows a daily cycle. Many people notice their bowels are most active in the morning, particularly after waking and after the first meal or warm drink. Regular routines reinforce that pattern, and erratic ones blur it.</p><p>This is why constipation is so common when travelling: new time zones, odd mealtimes, less movement and less privacy all disrupt the signals the gut relies on.</p></section><section id="what-throws-it-off"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Throws the Rhythm Off</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Irregular meals or skipped breakfasts</li><li>Too little fibre or water</li><li>Long periods of sitting</li><li>Ignoring the urge to go because you are busy</li><li>Poor or irregular sleep</li><li>Stress and anxiety</li></ul><p>Some medicines, including certain painkillers and iron supplements, can also cause constipation, so it is worth asking a pharmacist if things changed after starting something new.</p></section><section id="rebuilding-rhythm"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Rebuilding the Rhythm</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Start with the morning, because that is when the gut is most ready to work. Wake at a similar time each day, drink a glass of warm water, move gently, and eat a regular breakfast. Then give yourself unhurried time afterwards rather than rushing out of the door.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>A consistent wake time</li><li>Warm water and gentle movement first thing</li><li>A regular breakfast</li><li>Unhurried time, not a race to leave</li></ul></div></section><section id="a-morning-sequence"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Short Morning Sequence</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Cat-cow (1 minute):</strong> slow rounds to wake the spine and abdomen.</li><li><strong>Knees-to-chest (1 minute):</strong> rock gently side to side.</li><li><strong>Supine twist (1 minute each side).</strong></li><li><strong>Low squat (Malasana, 1 minute):</strong> with heels on a rolled blanket if needed.</li><li><strong>Belly breathing (3 minutes):</strong> slow breaths that let the abdomen move freely.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Many people find a footstool under the feet on the toilet helps, as it brings the body closer to a squatting position.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is it normal not to go every day?</h3><p>Yes. Normal ranges from several times a day to a few times a week. What matters most is what is normal for you, and whether that changes.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Does warm water in the morning really help?</h3><p>Many people find a warm drink on waking encourages a bowel movement, partly by stimulating the gut&rsquo;s natural morning activity. It is a simple habit worth trying.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Which yoga pose helps constipation most?</h3><p>There is no single best pose, but knees-to-chest, gentle twists and a supported squat are popular and comfortable. Regular daily movement matters more than any one posture.</p></section><section id="get-checked"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to Get Checked</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Occasional constipation is very common. See a doctor if it lasts more than a few weeks, alternates with diarrhoea, or comes with blood, weight loss, severe pain or a lasting change from your normal pattern. These deserve a proper look.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Your Bowels Have a Rhythm: How Yoga Helps You Get It Back', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Your-Bowels-Have' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Yoga-Based-Guide-to-Digestive-Health
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Your gut is talking to you all the time. Heartburn after a rushed lunch, bloating on stressful days, sluggishness after poor sleep: these are signals, not random annoyances. Yoga is, at heart, a practice of paying attention, which makes it a natural way to start listening and responding.</p><section id="the-signals"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>The Signals Worth Noticing</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Digestive symptoms often follow patterns. Notice when they happen and what came before: what you ate, how quickly you ate it, how you slept, how stressed you felt. Over a couple of weeks, the patterns usually become clear.</p><ul class="art-list"><li>Bloating or discomfort that follows certain meals</li><li>Symptoms that flare on stressful days</li><li>Changes linked to poor sleep or travel</li><li>Heartburn when eating late or lying down soon after</li></ul></section><section id="gut-and-mind"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>The Gut and the Mind Are Connected</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The gut and brain are in constant two-way communication through nerves and chemical messengers. Stress can make the gut more sensitive and change how fast things move through it; an unsettled gut can, in turn, affect mood and anxiety.</p><p>This connection is why calming practices often help digestion even though they never touch food. A calmer nervous system gives the gut the conditions it needs to work well.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;Digestion is not only what you eat. It is the state you are in when you eat it.&rdquo;</p></div></section><section id="yogic-habits"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Yogic Habits for Better Digestion</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Traditional yogic advice on eating is simple and still sensible:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Eat mindfully:</strong> sit down, slow down and chew properly.</li><li><strong>Do not overfill:</strong> stopping before you feel stuffed helps comfort.</li><li><strong>Keep regular mealtimes,</strong> and leave a few hours between dinner and sleep.</li><li><strong>Favour fresh, simple, home-cooked food</strong> over heavily processed meals.</li><li><strong>Pause before eating:</strong> a few calm breaths shift the body towards rest-and-digest.</li></ul></section><section id="a-practice-for-digestion"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>A Practice for Digestion</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Diaphragmatic breathing (3 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Cat-cow (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Seated forward fold, gently (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Supine twist (1 minute each side)</strong></li><li><strong>Wind-relieving pose (1 minute)</strong></li><li><strong>Vajrasana after meals:</strong> sitting on the heels for a few minutes after eating is a traditional practice many people find comfortable.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Keep vigorous practice for an empty stomach, and leave at least two hours after a main meal before anything strenuous.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can I do yoga straight after eating?</h3><p>Gentle breathing or sitting in Vajrasana is fine. Wait about two hours after a main meal before stronger postures, twists or inversions.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">What is mindful eating?</h3><p>Eating slowly and with attention: sitting down, noticing taste and texture, chewing well and stopping before you are uncomfortably full. It is one of the simplest yogic habits for better digestion.</p></section><section id="listen-to-red-flags"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Listening Includes Knowing When to Get Help</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Listening to your gut also means taking warning signs seriously. Blood in the stool, unexplained weight loss, persistent vomiting, difficulty swallowing, severe pain or a lasting change in bowel habits all need a doctor, not just a new routine.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Your Gut Is Trying to Tell You Something: A Yoga Guide to Digestion', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Yoga-Based-Guide-to-Digestive-Health' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- Your-Gut-Is-not-Upset
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">You eat the same meal on a calm Sunday and a frantic Monday, and only one of them leaves you cramped and uncomfortable. That is not your imagination. How you feel has a direct effect on how your gut behaves, and for many people stress is as big a trigger as any food.</p><section id="the-gut-brain-link"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>The Gut-Brain Connection</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The gut has its own extensive network of nerves and is linked to the brain through the vagus nerve and a constant flow of chemical signals. When the brain senses threat, it diverts resources away from digestion. That is useful if you are running from danger; it is less useful when the &lsquo;danger&rsquo; is an inbox.</p></section><section id="how-stress-shows-up"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Stress Shows Up in the Gut</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Butterflies, nausea or a knotted stomach</li><li>Cramping and urgency, or the opposite, everything slowing down</li><li>Bloating and increased sensitivity to normal gas</li><li>Heartburn and indigestion</li><li>Loss of appetite, or eating fast without noticing</li></ul><p>For people with irritable bowel syndrome in particular, stress and anxiety are among the most common triggers of flare-ups.</p></section><section id="calming-the-system"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Calming the System, Not Just the Stomach</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Because the trigger is the nervous system, the response has to reach the nervous system too. This is where yoga is genuinely useful. Slow breathing with a longer exhale, gentle movement and deep relaxation all encourage the body back towards its rest-and-digest state.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Breathe out longer than you breathe in</li><li>Move gently rather than intensely when stressed</li><li>Make meals a pause, not a task done at your desk</li></ul></div></section><section id="practices-for-a-nervous-gut"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Practices for a Nervous Gut</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Box breathing:</strong> in for four, hold for four, out for four, hold for four, for a few minutes.</li><li><strong>Belly breathing</strong> with a hand on the abdomen.</li><li><strong>Supported child&rsquo;s pose,</strong> which many people find settling.</li><li><strong>Legs up the wall</strong> for five minutes before an evening meal.</li><li><strong>Yoga Nidra</strong> or a guided relaxation before sleep.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Try three slow breaths before every meal. It sounds small, but it shifts the body towards digestion before the first bite.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can anxiety really cause stomach problems?</h3><p>Yes. Anxiety and stress affect how the gut moves and how sensitive it feels, which can cause cramps, nausea, urgency, bloating or constipation.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How do I know if it&rsquo;s stress or something I ate?</h3><p>Keep a simple diary of meals, symptoms and stress levels for a couple of weeks. If symptoms track your stress more closely than your food, the nervous system is likely a big part of the picture.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Will calming my mind fix my gut completely?</h3><p>For some people it makes a large difference; for others it is one part of the answer. If symptoms persist, see a doctor to rule out other causes.</p></section><section id="both-matter"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Food and Feelings Both Matter</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">None of this means food does not matter. Some people do have genuine intolerances or conditions that need diagnosis and specific changes. The point is that if you have only ever looked at your plate, you may be missing half the picture.</p><p>If symptoms are persistent, severe, or come with warning signs such as blood, weight loss or a lasting change in bowel habits, please see a doctor.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Your Gut Isn''t Upset Because of What You Ate: It''s How You Feel', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'Your-Gut-Is-not-Upset' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- yoga-for-blood-sugar-control-and-stability
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Stable blood sugar is not only about what you eat. Movement, sleep and stress all push glucose up or down through the day. Yoga touches all three, which is why it can be a useful part of keeping levels steadier, alongside diet, medication where needed and regular check-ups.</p><section id="why-levels-swing"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Blood Sugar Swings</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Blood sugar rises after meals, especially those rich in refined carbohydrates, and falls as the body moves glucose into the cells. When the body does not respond well to insulin, or does not make enough of it, those swings become larger and last longer.</p><p>Stress plays a bigger role than many people realise. Stress hormones such as cortisol and adrenaline release stored glucose into the blood, preparing you for action. If the stress is constant, so is that extra push.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Can Help</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Muscle activity:</strong> working muscles take up glucose from the blood, and regular activity can improve how well the body responds to insulin.</li><li><strong>Stress reduction:</strong> calming practices can lower the stress hormones that raise glucose.</li><li><strong>Better sleep:</strong> poor sleep is linked to poorer blood sugar control, and relaxing evening practice can help sleep.</li><li><strong>Awareness:</strong> regular practice often makes people more attentive to hunger, fullness and energy.</li></ul><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Yoga supports, it does not replace, diet and medication</li><li>Consistency matters more than intensity</li><li>Pair practice with regular glucose monitoring if advised</li></ul></div></section><section id="practices"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Practices to Include</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A balanced practice combines some movement that uses the large muscles with breathing and relaxation:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Sun salutations</strong> at a gentle, steady pace</li><li><strong>Standing postures</strong> such as warrior poses and chair pose</li><li><strong>Seated twists and forward folds,</strong> done gently</li><li><strong>Slow breathing</strong> with a longer exhale</li><li><strong>Final relaxation</strong> of at least five minutes</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you take insulin or medicines that can cause low blood sugar, check with your doctor before starting a new practice, keep a quick source of sugar nearby, and stop if you feel shaky, sweaty, dizzy or confused.</p></div></section><section id="after-meals"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Movement After Meals</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A short walk or some gentle movement after eating can help the body use glucose from the meal. Even ten to fifteen minutes makes a difference for many people. Save strenuous practice for when you are not straight off a heavy meal.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga lower blood sugar immediately?</h3><p>Physical activity can lower blood sugar during and after a session as muscles use glucose. The effect varies, so if you have diabetes, check your levels before and after while you learn how your body responds.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How often should I practise for blood sugar benefits?</h3><p>Regularity matters most. Aim for several sessions a week plus daily movement, such as a walk after meals, rather than occasional long sessions.</p></section><section id="work-with-your-doctor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Work With Your Care Team</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">If you have diabetes or prediabetes, keep your doctor informed when you change your activity, as medication doses sometimes need adjusting as control improves. Yoga is a good partner to medical care, not a substitute for it.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Blood Sugar Control: Keep Glucose Levels Stable Naturally', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-blood-sugar-control-and-stability' AND `content` LIKE '%Stress has become an unavoidable part of modern life%';

-- what-yoga-can-and-cannot-do-for-diabetes
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">There is a lot of hopeful talk about yoga and diabetes, and some of it goes too far. Yoga can genuinely help people living with diabetes. It cannot cure the condition, and it should never be used in place of medication or medical advice. Setting honest expectations helps you get the real benefits safely.</p><section id="what-it-can-do"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Yoga Can Do</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Support blood sugar management</strong> as part of regular physical activity</li><li><strong>Reduce stress,</strong> which can otherwise push glucose levels up</li><li><strong>Improve sleep,</strong> which is linked to better glucose control</li><li><strong>Help with weight management</strong> alongside diet and other exercise</li><li><strong>Improve flexibility, balance and wellbeing,</strong> which matter for long-term quality of life</li></ul><p>Many people also find that a regular practice helps them stick to other healthy habits, from eating more mindfully to taking medication consistently.</p></section><section id="what-it-cannot-do"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Yoga Cannot Do</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>It cannot cure type 1 diabetes.</strong> People with type 1 need insulin, always.</li><li><strong>It does not replace medication</strong> for type 2 diabetes. Any change to medication must come from your doctor.</li><li><strong>It cannot undo the effects of a poor diet</strong> on its own.</li><li><strong>It cannot reverse established complications</strong> such as nerve or eye damage.</li></ul><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;The most dangerous claim in wellness is &lsquo;you can stop your medication&rsquo;. Yoga never needs you to.&rdquo;</p></div></section><section id="realistic-picture"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Realistic Picture</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">For type 2 diabetes, lifestyle changes, including regular activity, diet and weight management, can make a significant difference, and some people are able to reduce medication under medical supervision. Yoga can be one of those activities. The results come from the whole package, sustained over time.</p></section><section id="practise-safely"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Practising Safely</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><div class="art-tip"><p>If you take insulin or medicines that can cause low blood sugar, check with your doctor before starting a new practice, keep a quick source of sugar nearby, and stop if you feel shaky, sweaty, dizzy or confused.</p></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Check your feet regularly if you have reduced sensation, as barefoot practice can hide small injuries.</li><li>If you have diabetic eye disease, ask your doctor before strong inversions or straining.</li><li>Stay hydrated and avoid practising in excessive heat.</li><li>Tell your yoga teacher about your condition.</li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga reverse type 2 diabetes?</h3><p>Lifestyle change, including weight loss, diet and activity, can bring some people&rsquo;s blood sugar back into a normal range, sometimes called remission. Yoga can be part of that, but it is the whole package, guided by your doctor, that makes the difference.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is yoga safe if I have diabetic complications?</h3><p>Usually, with adaptations. Talk to your doctor first if you have eye disease, nerve damage in the feet, or heart problems, and let your teacher know so they can modify postures.</p></section><section id="bottom-line"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>The Bottom Line</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga is a valuable, gentle, sustainable form of activity for people with diabetes, with real benefits for stress, sleep and wellbeing. Treat it as one important tool among several, and keep your care team informed.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>A helpful support, not a cure</li><li>Never stop medication without your doctor</li><li>Benefits come from consistency</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'What Yoga Can and Cannot Do for Diabetes: Honest Expectations', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'what-yoga-can-and-cannot-do-for-diabetes' AND `content` LIKE '%clear limits of yoga in heart disease%';

-- yoga-vs-medication-vs-exercise-for-diabetes
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">People often ask which is best for diabetes: yoga, exercise or medication. It is the wrong question. Each does something different, and they work best together. Understanding the role of each helps you build a plan with your doctor rather than choosing one and hoping.</p><section id="medication"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Medication Does</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Diabetes medicines work in different ways: some help the body make or use insulin more effectively, some reduce the glucose the liver releases, and insulin itself replaces what the body cannot make. For people with type 1 diabetes, insulin is essential. For many with type 2, medication is an important part of keeping blood sugar in a safe range and preventing complications.</p><p>Medication is prescribed and adjusted by your doctor. It should never be stopped or changed based on how you feel after exercise or yoga.</p></section><section id="exercise"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Exercise Does</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Regular physical activity helps muscles take up glucose and can improve insulin sensitivity. A mix of aerobic activity, such as brisk walking or cycling, and strength training is widely recommended for people with type 2 diabetes. Both help with weight management and heart health, which matters because diabetes raises cardiovascular risk.</p></section><section id="yoga"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>What Yoga Adds</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga is a form of exercise, but it brings something extra: a strong focus on breathing, relaxation and attention. That makes it particularly useful for the stress and sleep side of diabetes, which ordinary workouts do not always address. Stronger styles also build some strength and stamina.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Medication: controls glucose directly</li><li>Exercise: improves insulin sensitivity and heart health</li><li>Yoga: activity plus stress and sleep support</li></ul></div></section><section id="working-together"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>How They Work Together</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">A sensible plan for many people combines medication as prescribed, regular aerobic exercise, some strength work, and yoga for flexibility, balance and stress. Yoga can even count towards your weekly activity, particularly more active styles.</p><p>As activity increases and control improves, medication needs can change, which is exactly why your doctor needs to stay involved.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Keep a simple log of your activity alongside your glucose readings. It helps you and your doctor see what is working.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Does yoga count as exercise for diabetes?</h3><p>Yes. More active styles count towards your weekly activity, and all styles add stress and sleep benefits. Many people combine yoga with walking and some strength work.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can I reduce my medication if I exercise more?</h3><p>Possibly, but only with your doctor. As activity improves your control, doses sometimes need lowering to avoid low blood sugar, which is exactly why you should never adjust them yourself.</p></section><section id="choosing-what-fits"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Choosing What Fits Your Life</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">The best plan is the one you will keep doing. If you love walking, walk. If yoga is what gets you moving, build around it. Talk to your doctor about what is safe for you, especially if you have complications or take medicines that can cause low blood sugar.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga vs Medication vs Exercise for Diabetes: How They Work Together', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-vs-medication-vs-exercise-for-diabetes' AND `content` LIKE '%than those without PCOS%';

-- yoga-for-diabetes-blood-sugar-management
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Living with diabetes means managing many things at once: food, activity, medication, stress, sleep. Yoga cannot manage diabetes for you, but it can make several of those things easier. This guide covers how it helps, what to practise, and how to do it safely.</p><section id="metabolic-wellness"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Diabetes and Metabolic Health</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Metabolic health describes how well the body handles energy, including blood sugar, blood fats, blood pressure and body weight. Diabetes affects all of these, and they affect each other. Supporting metabolic health means looking after the whole system, not just one number.</p></section><section id="benefits"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Supports It</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Physical activity</strong> that helps the body use glucose</li><li><strong>Stress relief,</strong> reducing hormones that raise blood sugar</li><li><strong>Better sleep,</strong> which supports glucose control and appetite regulation</li><li><strong>Strength and flexibility,</strong> making other activity easier</li><li><strong>Mindfulness,</strong> which many people find helps with eating habits</li></ul></section><section id="a-weekly-plan"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Simple Weekly Plan</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Three or more days:</strong> a 30 to 45 minute practice including gentle sun salutations, standing postures and relaxation.</li><li><strong>Most days:</strong> a short walk after main meals.</li><li><strong>Every evening:</strong> five to ten minutes of slow breathing or relaxation before bed.</li></ul><p>Build up gradually. If you are new to activity, start with shorter sessions and increase as you feel able.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>If you take insulin or medicines that can cause low blood sugar, check with your doctor before starting a new practice, keep a quick source of sugar nearby, and stop if you feel shaky, sweaty, dizzy or confused.</p></div></section><section id="helpful-postures"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Helpful Postures</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Mountain pose and gentle standing postures</strong> for strength and balance</li><li><strong>Chair pose</strong> to work the large leg muscles</li><li><strong>Cat-cow and gentle twists</strong> for spinal mobility</li><li><strong>Bridge pose</strong> for the legs and back</li><li><strong>Legs up the wall</strong> for relaxation</li></ul><p>Modify anything that is uncomfortable, and use props such as a chair or blocks freely.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Which yoga style is best for diabetes?</h3><p>A balanced style that includes some active postures, breathing and relaxation, such as Hatha yoga, suits most people. The best style is the one you can practise regularly and safely.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How long before I see benefits?</h3><p>Some people notice better energy, sleep or stress levels within a few weeks. Changes in longer-term blood sugar measures take months of consistent practice alongside your other treatment.</p></section><section id="whole-picture"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Keeping the Whole Picture in View</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga sits alongside a balanced diet, prescribed medication, regular monitoring and check-ups for your eyes, feet, kidneys and heart. Keep your doctor informed as your activity changes.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Support the whole metabolic system</li><li>Little and often beats occasional and intense</li><li>Stay connected with your care team</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Diabetes: Natural Support for Blood Sugar and Metabolic Health', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-diabetes-blood-sugar-management' AND `content` LIKE '%small butterfly-shaped gland in the neck%';

-- yoga-for-type-2-diabetes-management
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Type 2 diabetes is strongly influenced by everyday life: how much we move, how we eat, how we sleep and how much stress we carry. That is frustrating, but it is also hopeful, because it means daily habits can make a real difference. Yoga offers a gentle, sustainable way to build several of those habits at once.</p><section id="move-more"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Move More</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Long periods of sitting are hard on blood sugar. Breaking them up with movement, and adding regular sessions of activity, helps the muscles use glucose and can improve insulin sensitivity over time.</p><p>Yoga is a good entry point for people who find gyms intimidating or who are carrying joint pain or extra weight. It can be adapted to almost any starting point, including chair-based practice.</p></section><section id="stress-less"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Stress Less</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Chronic stress keeps stress hormones elevated, and those hormones raise blood sugar. Stress also tends to disrupt sleep and push us towards comfort eating. Yoga&rsquo;s breathing and relaxation practices are among the most practical tools for bringing that load down.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Five minutes of slow breathing, breathing out for longer than you breathe in, is a simple daily stress reset you can do anywhere.</p></div></section><section id="live-better"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Live Better</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Beyond the numbers, people with type 2 diabetes often report that regular yoga improves energy, mood, mobility and confidence in their bodies. Those things matter in their own right, and they make it easier to keep up healthy habits.</p></section><section id="getting-started"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Getting Started</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Begin with a beginner or gentle class, or a short routine at home.</li><li>Aim for consistency: two or three sessions a week to start.</li><li>Use a chair or wall for support if balance is a concern.</li><li>Add a short walk after meals on other days.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>If you take insulin or medicines that can cause low blood sugar, speak to your doctor before starting, and keep a quick source of sugar with you while you practise.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">I&rsquo;m overweight and stiff. Can I still do yoga?</h3><p>Yes. Yoga can be adapted to any body, including chair-based practice. Start gently, use props, and build up gradually.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is walking or yoga better for type 2 diabetes?</h3><p>Both are helpful and they complement each other. Walking is excellent for blood sugar after meals; yoga adds strength, flexibility, balance and stress relief.</p></section><section id="with-your-doctor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Alongside Your Treatment</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga does not replace medication or medical advice. Many people with type 2 diabetes improve their control through lifestyle changes, and some reduce medication under medical supervision, but any change must be made with your doctor.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Break up sitting time</li><li>Use breathing to manage stress</li><li>Start gently and stay consistent</li><li>Keep your doctor involved</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Type 2 Diabetes: Move More, Stress Less, Live Better', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-type-2-diabetes-management' AND `content` LIKE '%small butterfly-shaped gland in the neck%';

-- yoga-for-diabetes-related-fatigue
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Tiredness is one of the most common and least talked-about parts of living with diabetes. Blood sugar swings, poor sleep, the mental load of managing a condition every day: it adds up. Gentle yoga will not fix the underlying causes, but it can help restore energy in a way that does not drain you further.</p><section id="why-so-tired"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Diabetes Can Make You Tired</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>High blood sugar</strong> can leave you sluggish because cells are not getting energy efficiently.</li><li><strong>Low blood sugar</strong> episodes can leave you washed out afterwards.</li><li><strong>Poor sleep,</strong> including from night-time trips to the toilet or sleep apnoea, which is more common in type 2 diabetes.</li><li><strong>Stress and emotional load</strong> of daily management.</li><li><strong>Other conditions</strong> such as anaemia, thyroid problems or depression, which can occur alongside diabetes.</li></ul><p>Because fatigue can have several causes, it is worth mentioning to your doctor rather than assuming it is just part of life.</p></section><section id="gentle-energy"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>Gentle Practice for Energy</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">When you are tired, an intense workout can feel impossible and may leave you more depleted. Gentle yoga works differently: it gets the blood moving, wakes up stiff muscles and calms the nervous system without demanding a lot.</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Gentle sun salutations</strong> at a slow pace</li><li><strong>Standing postures held briefly</strong> for strength</li><li><strong>Cat-cow and gentle backbends</strong> to open the chest</li><li><strong>Seated twists</strong></li><li><strong>Short relaxation</strong> to finish</li></ul></section><section id="breathing-for-energy"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Breathing for Energy</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Slow, full breathing can feel surprisingly refreshing. Try sitting tall, breathing in through the nose for a count of four, and out for a count of four, for two or three minutes. Avoid forceful or rapid breathing techniques if you feel lightheaded.</p><div class="art-tip"><p>Practise when your blood sugar is in your usual safe range, keep a quick source of sugar nearby, and stop if you feel shaky or faint.</p></div></section><section id="rest-and-sleep"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Rest and Sleep</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Better sleep is one of the most powerful ways to improve energy. A short, calming practice in the evening, such as legs up the wall, gentle stretches and slow breathing, can help the body settle. Keep regular sleep and wake times where possible.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I exercise when I&rsquo;m exhausted?</h3><p>Gentle movement often helps more than rest alone, but listen to your body. On very low days, breathing and restorative postures are enough. If fatigue is persistent or severe, see your doctor.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga help me sleep better with diabetes?</h3><p>Many people find a short, calming evening practice helps them settle. Good blood sugar control overnight and treating issues like sleep apnoea also make a big difference.</p></section><section id="talk-to-your-doctor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to Talk to Your Doctor</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Persistent or worsening fatigue, especially with other symptoms such as excessive thirst, frequent urination, breathlessness, low mood or weight changes, should be discussed with your doctor. It may mean your treatment needs adjusting or that something else needs checking.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Fatigue is common, but worth investigating</li><li>Gentle practice restores rather than drains</li><li>Sleep is part of the treatment</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Diabetes-Related Fatigue: Restore Your Energy Gently', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-diabetes-related-fatigue' AND `content` LIKE '%Your heart races for no reason%';

-- limits-of-yoga-immune-disorders
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Autoimmune and other immune disorders, such as rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, multiple sclerosis or inflammatory bowel disease, are serious conditions that need medical care. Yoga can be a genuinely helpful companion to that care. It cannot treat the disease itself, and believing otherwise can be harmful.</p><section id="what-immune-disorders-are"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Immune Disorders Are</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">In autoimmune conditions, the immune system mistakenly attacks the body&rsquo;s own tissues. Other immune disorders involve the immune system being underactive. These conditions vary widely, often come in flares, and are usually managed with specialist medicines that calm or adjust the immune response.</p></section><section id="what-yoga-can-support"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Yoga Can Support</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Stress management:</strong> many people notice stress can coincide with flares, and calming practices help them cope.</li><li><strong>Mobility and strength:</strong> gentle, adapted movement helps maintain function, particularly for joint-related conditions.</li><li><strong>Fatigue and sleep:</strong> restorative practice can make fatigue easier to live with.</li><li><strong>Pain coping:</strong> breathing and relaxation can change how pain is experienced, even when they do not remove it.</li><li><strong>Emotional wellbeing</strong> and a sense of control over something in daily life.</li></ul></section><section id="where-medicine-leads"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Where Medicine Must Lead</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Yoga cannot switch off an overactive immune system, reverse tissue damage, or replace disease-modifying treatments. &lsquo;Immune-boosting&rsquo; claims make little sense here: in autoimmune disease, the problem is not a weak immune system but a misdirected one.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;In immune disorders, yoga can help you live better with the condition. Medicine is what treats the condition.&rdquo;</p></div><p>Never stop or reduce prescribed medication because you feel better after practice. Flares can be serious, and treatment decisions belong with your specialist.</p></section><section id="practising-safely"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Practising Safely</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>During a flare, rest or switch to breathing and very gentle movement.</li><li>Protect inflamed joints: avoid weight-bearing on them and use props generously.</li><li>Avoid overheating, which can worsen symptoms in some conditions, including MS.</li><li>If you take medicines that suppress the immune system, avoid crowded classes when you are particularly vulnerable to infection.</li><li>Tell your teacher about your condition.</li></ul></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga boost my immune system?</h3><p>In autoimmune conditions, the immune system is not too weak but misdirected, so &lsquo;boosting&rsquo; is not the goal. Yoga&rsquo;s value lies in stress management, movement, sleep and wellbeing.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Should I practise during a flare?</h3><p>Usually only very gently, if at all. Rest, breathing and comfortable movement are best during flares; save stronger practice for when symptoms settle, and follow your specialist&rsquo;s advice.</p></section><section id="working-together"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Working With Your Care Team</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Ask your doctor or physiotherapist what kind of movement suits your condition. Many specialists welcome gentle activity, and some can point you towards adapted classes. Used this way, yoga becomes a steady, supportive part of your life rather than a risky substitute for care.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>A helpful companion, not a treatment</li><li>Never adjust medication on your own</li><li>Adapt practice to flares</li><li>Keep your specialist informed</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Limits of Yoga in Immune Disorders: What It Can and Cannot Do', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'limits-of-yoga-immune-disorders' AND `content` LIKE '%These are not random problems%';

-- yoga-for-bone-health-joint-support-menopause
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">After menopause, falling oestrogen levels speed up bone loss, and many women also notice stiffer, achier joints. Strong bones and steady balance become more important every year. Yoga can be part of looking after both, as long as it is the right kind of practice and you know which movements need care.</p><section id="why-bones-change"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why Bones Change After 40</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Oestrogen helps protect bone. As it declines through perimenopause and menopause, bone density tends to fall more quickly, raising the risk of osteoporosis and fractures in later life. Joints can also feel stiffer, partly through hormonal change and partly through age and lower activity.</p></section><section id="what-builds-bone"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Actually Builds Bone</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Bones respond to load. Weight-bearing exercise and strength training send the signal bones need to stay strong. Yoga postures that hold your body weight, such as standing poses, plank variations and supported arm balances, provide some of this load, and they also build the muscle that protects joints.</p><p>Yoga is best seen as one part of a bone-healthy routine that may also include walking, strength training, enough calcium and vitamin D, and not smoking.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Weight-bearing postures load the bones</li><li>Strong muscles protect joints</li><li>Balance work reduces fall risk</li></ul></div></section><section id="helpful-postures"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Helpful Postures</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Warrior I and II</strong> for leg and hip strength</li><li><strong>Tree pose and other balances,</strong> near a wall at first</li><li><strong>Chair pose</strong> for the large leg muscles</li><li><strong>Plank on the knees or forearms</strong> for upper body and core</li><li><strong>Bridge pose</strong> for the back of the body</li><li><strong>Gentle backbends</strong> such as sphinx, which support good posture</li></ul></section><section id="movements-to-modify"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Movements to Modify</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">If you have osteoporosis or low bone density, some movements need care because they place stress on the spine:</p><ul class="art-list"><li>Deep forward bends with a rounded spine</li><li>Strong or forced twists</li><li>Full inversions such as headstand and shoulder stand</li><li>Sit-ups and crunch-like movements</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Ask your doctor whether you have had a bone density scan. If you have osteoporosis, look for a teacher experienced in adapting yoga for it.</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga increase bone density?</h3><p>Weight-bearing postures can help maintain bone, and some small studies suggest benefits, but yoga alone is unlikely to be enough. Combine it with strength training, walking and good nutrition.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is yoga safe if I have osteoporosis?</h3><p>It can be, with the right adaptations. Avoid deep rounded forward bends, forced twists and full inversions, and work with a teacher experienced in osteoporosis.</p></section><section id="joint-comfort"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Comfort for Stiff Joints</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Gentle movement through a comfortable range each day often eases stiffness. Warm up before stronger poses, use props freely, and avoid pushing into pain. Sharp or lasting joint pain, swelling or a sudden loss of movement should be checked by a doctor.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Bone Health and Joint Support in Menopause', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-bone-health-joint-support-menopause' AND `content` LIKE '%These are not random problems%';

-- yoga-for-mood-changes-emotional-balance-menopause
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Irritability, low mood, anxiety, tearfulness, feeling unlike yourself: emotional changes are a real and common part of the menopause transition. Hormones play a part, but they are rarely the whole story. Sleep, stress, life changes and how we feel about ageing all feed in. Yoga can help with several of these at once.</p><section id="more-than-hormones"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>More Than Hormones</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Fluctuating oestrogen and progesterone can affect mood directly. At the same time, menopause often arrives in a busy, demanding stage of life: ageing parents, teenagers or children leaving home, career pressures. Add broken sleep from night sweats, and it is no surprise that emotional balance can feel harder to hold.</p><p>Recognising this helps. It means that hormones are not the only lever; sleep, stress and support matter too.</p></section><section id="how-yoga-helps"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>How Yoga Helps</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Calming the stress response</strong> through slow breathing and relaxation</li><li><strong>Improving sleep,</strong> which has a strong effect on mood</li><li><strong>Movement,</strong> which is known to support mood and wellbeing</li><li><strong>Time for yourself</strong> in a stage of life when there is often very little</li><li><strong>Community,</strong> if you practise in a class with others</li></ul></section><section id="practices-for-balance"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Practices for Emotional Balance</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Cooling breath (Sheetali or Sheetkari):</strong> breathing in over the tongue or through the teeth, which many women find soothing during hot flushes and irritability.</li><li><strong>Alternate nostril breathing</strong> for a steadier mind.</li><li><strong>Gentle heart-opening postures</strong> such as supported fish pose.</li><li><strong>Restorative poses</strong> with props, held for several minutes.</li><li><strong>Yoga Nidra</strong> for deep rest.</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>On difficult days, choose calming practice over vigorous practice. Strong, heating sequences can sometimes make irritability and hot flushes worse.</p></div></section><section id="be-kind-to-yourself"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Be Kind to Yourself</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Mood changes in menopause are not a personal failing. Talking honestly with friends, a partner or other women going through the same transition can lift a lot of weight.</p><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;You are not becoming someone else. You are moving through a change, and you are allowed support while you do.&rdquo;</p></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga help with menopause anxiety?</h3><p>Many women find breathing practices and gentle yoga reduce anxiety and help them feel steadier. If anxiety is severe or persistent, speak to your doctor about further support.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Is it normal to feel angry or tearful in menopause?</h3><p>Yes, emotional changes are common during the transition. You do not have to simply put up with them; support and treatments are available.</p></section><section id="when-to-seek-help"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to Seek Help</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">If low mood lasts more than a couple of weeks, affects daily life, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, please speak to a doctor promptly. There are effective treatments, including hormone therapy for some women and talking therapies, and yoga works well alongside them.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Mood Changes and Emotional Balance During Menopause', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-mood-changes-emotional-balance-menopause' AND `content` LIKE '%These are not random problems%';

-- limits-of-yoga-managing-menopause-symptoms
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Yoga is often recommended for menopause, and for good reason: it can help with sleep, stress, mood and fitness. But it is not a treatment for every symptom, and it should not stop anyone from getting medical support. Knowing where the limits are helps you use yoga well and seek help when you need it.</p><section id="what-it-can-help"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>What Yoga Can Help With</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Stress and anxiety,</strong> through breathing and relaxation</li><li><strong>Sleep quality,</strong> for many women</li><li><strong>Mood and wellbeing</strong></li><li><strong>Strength, balance and flexibility,</strong> which support long-term health</li><li><strong>Coping with hot flushes:</strong> calming practice may make them feel less distressing, even if it does not stop them</li></ul></section><section id="what-it-cannot-do"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What It Cannot Do</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>It cannot replace hormones.</strong> Yoga does not restore oestrogen levels.</li><li><strong>It cannot reliably stop severe hot flushes and night sweats.</strong> Medical treatments, including hormone therapy where suitable, are far more effective for many women.</li><li><strong>It cannot prevent osteoporosis on its own.</strong> Bone health also needs strength work, nutrition and sometimes medication.</li><li><strong>It cannot treat vaginal dryness or urinary symptoms,</strong> which often respond well to specific treatments.</li><li><strong>It cannot diagnose anything.</strong> Some symptoms that seem menopausal have other causes.</li></ul><div class="art-pull"><p>&ldquo;Choosing yoga should never mean choosing to suffer through symptoms that could be treated.&rdquo;</p></div></section><section id="signs-to-check"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>Symptoms That Need a Doctor</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Any bleeding after your periods have stopped for a year</li><li>Very heavy, prolonged or irregular bleeding during perimenopause</li><li>Severe low mood or anxiety</li><li>Palpitations, chest pain or breathlessness</li><li>Symptoms that are severely affecting work, sleep or relationships</li></ul></section><section id="best-of-both"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Getting the Best of Both</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Many women find the best results come from combining approaches: medical treatment where it helps, and yoga for the wellbeing, strength and calm that medicine does not provide. A doctor who understands menopause can talk you through your options, including whether hormone therapy is suitable for you.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Yoga supports wellbeing, it does not replace hormones</li><li>Effective treatments exist, so ask about them</li><li>Some symptoms always need checking</li></ul></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Does yoga stop hot flushes?</h3><p>It is unlikely to stop them, though calming practices may help some women cope with them better. For frequent or severe flushes, talk to your doctor about treatment options.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can I do yoga and take HRT?</h3><p>Yes. The two work well together: hormone therapy treats symptoms, and yoga supports strength, balance, sleep and wellbeing.</p></section><section id="practising-well"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Practising Well Through Menopause</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Choose a practice that combines some strength and balance work with calming breathing and relaxation. Keep the room cool if you get hot flushes, and adapt your practice to how you feel each day.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Limits of Yoga for Menopause: What It Can and Cannot Do', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'limits-of-yoga-managing-menopause-symptoms' AND `content` LIKE '%These are not random problems%';

-- yoga-for-menopause-womens-hormonal-aging
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">Menopause is a natural transition, not an illness, but it can bring symptoms that make daily life harder. Yoga will not change your hormones. What it can do is support the body and mind through the change: steadier sleep, a calmer nervous system, stronger muscles and bones, and a better relationship with a body that is changing.</p><section id="the-transition"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Understanding the Transition</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Perimenopause, the years leading up to the last period, can begin in the forties and bring irregular cycles, hot flushes, sleep problems and mood changes. Menopause is confirmed after twelve months without a period. Afterwards, lower oestrogen affects bone density, heart health and the pelvic floor, which is why this stage matters for long-term health.</p></section><section id="what-works"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>What Actually Works</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Evidence and experience point to a few things that genuinely help:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Regular movement,</strong> including strength and weight-bearing exercise, for bones, muscle and heart</li><li><strong>Calming practices</strong> for stress, sleep and mood</li><li><strong>Good sleep habits</strong> and a cool bedroom</li><li><strong>Medical treatment</strong> where symptoms are troublesome, including hormone therapy for many women</li><li><strong>Pelvic floor exercises</strong> to support bladder health</li></ul><p>Yoga can contribute to the first three directly, and fits easily alongside the others.</p></section><section id="a-balanced-practice"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>A Balanced Practice</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Strength:</strong> standing postures, chair pose, plank variations</li><li><strong>Balance:</strong> tree pose and warrior III with support</li><li><strong>Mobility:</strong> cat-cow, gentle twists, hip openers</li><li><strong>Calm:</strong> slow breathing, cooling breath, supported restorative poses</li><li><strong>Rest:</strong> Yoga Nidra or a long final relaxation</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Two or three varied sessions a week, plus a few minutes of breathing daily, is a realistic and effective starting point.</p></div></section><section id="pelvic-floor"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Don&rsquo;t Forget the Pelvic Floor</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Lower oestrogen can weaken pelvic floor tissues, contributing to leaks when you cough, sneeze or jump. Gentle pelvic floor engagement can be woven into many yoga postures. If you have significant symptoms, a women&rsquo;s health physiotherapist can help.</p></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Can yoga balance hormones during menopause?</h3><p>Yoga does not restore oestrogen levels. It can help with the effects of hormonal change, such as stress, sleep and mood, and supports long-term health through strength and balance.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">How often should I practise during menopause?</h3><p>Two or three varied sessions a week, plus a few minutes of breathing on most days, is a realistic and effective start.</p></section><section id="ageing-well"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>Ageing Well, Not Just Getting Through</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Many women find that menopause becomes a turning point: a time to put their own health first. A steady yoga practice is a good way to build habits that will serve you for decades. Keep your doctor in the loop, especially about bone and heart health.</p><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Yoga supports the body through the change</li><li>Combine strength, balance and calm</li><li>Medical treatment and yoga can work together</li></ul></div><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Menopause and Hormonal Ageing: What Actually Works', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-menopause-womens-hormonal-aging' AND `content` LIKE '%These are not random problems%';

-- yoga-for-sleep-problems-during-menopause
UPDATE `articles` SET `content` = '<p class="art-lede">You fall asleep exhausted, then wake at 3am, hot, restless and wide awake. Sleep problems are one of the most common and draining parts of menopause. Understanding why they happen makes it easier to find what helps, and gentle yoga is one of the simplest tools to add to an evening routine.</p><section id="why-you-cant-sleep"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">01</span>Why You Can&rsquo;t Sleep</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Night sweats and hot flushes</strong> wake you or make it hard to settle.</li><li><strong>Hormonal changes</strong> affect sleep regulation directly.</li><li><strong>Anxiety and a busy mind,</strong> often worse at night.</li><li><strong>Needing the toilet</strong> more often.</li><li><strong>Sleep apnoea,</strong> which becomes more common after menopause and is often missed in women.</li></ul><p>If you snore loudly, wake gasping, or feel exhausted however long you sleep, mention it to your doctor, as sleep apnoea is treatable.</p></section><section id="an-evening-routine"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">02</span>A Calming Evening Routine</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Practise this 30 to 60 minutes before bed, in a cool room with soft light:</p><ul class="art-list"><li><strong>Legs up the wall (5 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Supported child&rsquo;s pose (3 minutes)</strong></li><li><strong>Reclined butterfly</strong> with cushions under the knees (3 minutes)</li><li><strong>Cooling breath (2 minutes),</strong> helpful if you feel warm</li><li><strong>Slow breathing,</strong> breathing out longer than you breathe in (3 minutes)</li></ul><div class="art-tip"><p>Avoid vigorous, heating practice in the evening. Save stronger sessions for the morning or early afternoon.</p></div></section><section id="when-you-wake"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">03</span>When You Wake in the Night</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">Rather than lying there frustrated, try a simple body scan or slow breathing in bed. If you are still awake after about twenty minutes, get up, keep the lights low, do something quiet and return to bed when you feel sleepy. Keep the bedroom cool and use layers you can throw off easily.</p></section><section id="sleep-habits"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">04</span>Habits That Help</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><ul class="art-list"><li>Keep regular sleep and wake times</li><li>Limit caffeine after midday and keep alcohol low, as both disrupt sleep and alcohol can trigger flushes</li><li>Get daylight and movement during the day</li><li>Keep screens out of the last hour before bed</li></ul><div class="art-take"><h4>The heart of it</h4><ul><li>Cool, calm, consistent</li><li>Gentle practice in the evening, stronger in the morning</li><li>Check for sleep apnoea if sleep never refreshes you</li></ul></div></section><section id="common-questions"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">05</span>Common Questions</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">What is the best yoga pose for sleep?</h3><p>Legs up the wall is a favourite for many women because it is restful and easy to do. A short sequence of restorative poses and slow breathing usually works better than any single pose.</p><h3 style="font-family:''Playfair Display'',serif;font-size:19px;margin:22px 0 8px">Why do I wake at 3am during menopause?</h3><p>Night sweats, hormonal changes, needing the toilet and a busy mind are common reasons. If it happens most nights and affects your days, talk to your doctor.</p></section><section id="treatment-options"><h2 class="art-h2"><span class="n">06</span>When to Ask About Treatment</h2><div class="art-rule"></div><p class="drop">If sleep problems are persistent and affecting your daily life, talk to your doctor. Treating night sweats, including with hormone therapy where suitable, often improves sleep considerably, and cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia is very effective. Yoga works well alongside both.</p><div class="art-cta"><p>If this practice is speaking to you, there is no better place to deepen it than where yoga began. Come and study with us in Rishikesh.</p><a class="course-link" href="/yoga-teacher-training-course-rishikesh-india/">Explore Our Courses <svg width="18" height="10" viewBox="0 0 20 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M1 5h17M14 1l4 4-4 4"/></svg></a></div></section>', `title_tag` = 'Yoga for Sleep Problems During Menopause: What Actually Helps', `modified_date` = '2026-10-24'
WHERE `slug` = 'yoga-for-sleep-problems-during-menopause' AND `content` LIKE '%measurable benefits for thyroid health%';

